    "evidence_text": "Ribosome biogenesis: Achilles heel of cancer?\n\nSince then, pathologists have used nucleolar morphology to predict the clinical outcome [1].\n\nNucleolar morphology is altered due to the up-regulation of ribosomal gene transcription.\n\n5S rRNA is transcribed by RNA polymerase III in the nucleoplasm.\n\nThis provides new therapeutic opportunities.\n\nThese targeting modalities of these drugs also lead to toxicity in normal highly proliferating tissues.\n\nThis raises an important question.\n\nCan targeting ribosome biogenesis without DNA damage offer any therapeutic potential?\n\nThe production of ribosomes is ubiquitous and fundamental to life.\n\nExcessive activation of this mechanism impairs craniofacial development.\n\nReview article\n\nAcquired abnormalities in ribosome function have been implicated more broadly in human malignancies.\n\nRibosomal DNA copy number loss and sequence variation in cancer\n\nThe sequence can also be more variable in the cancer genome.\n\nCancer genomes with lower copies have mutational evidence of mTOR hyperactivity.\n\nLoss of copies occurs early and is associated with hypersensitivity to DNA damage.\n\nGrowth control and ribosomopathies.\n\nMoreover, defects in their molecular components can alter the pattern of gene expression.\n\nYet, evidence suggesting p53 and/or Hdm2 independent pathways also exists.",
